          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which anatomical landmarks define the two vertical lines used to divide the abdo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which anatomical landmarks define the two vertical lines used to divide the abdomen into the nine-region grid?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anterior superior iliac spines</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The anterior superior iliac spines are pelvic landmarks used for other measurements. The vertical lines of the nine-region grid descend from the mid-clavicular points — the midpoint of each clavicle.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mid-clavicular points</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Each vertical line runs down from the mid-clavicular point. These two verticals, crossed by the subcostal and transtubercular horizontal lines, create the nine named regions used to localise abdominal findings.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mid-axillary lines</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The mid-axillary lines are used in thoracic examination, not the abdominal grid. The two vertical lines of the nine-region grid descend from the mid-clavicular points.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lateral borders of the rectus abdominis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The lateral rectus borders (linea semilunaris) are surface landmarks but do not define the nine-region grid. The correct reference points are the mid-clavicular points of each clavicle.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The two vertical lines each descend from the mid-clavicular point, the midpoint of the clavicle. Together with the subcostal line (below the ribcage) and the transtubercular line (connecting the iliac tubercles), they form the nine-region grid."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The mid-clavicular points anchor the vertical lines of the grid — combined with the subcostal and transtubercular horizontal lines, they create the nine regions that form your abdominal examination map."
                }
              ],
