          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>When palpating for splenic enlargement, from which location should you begin?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>When palpating for splenic enlargement, from which location should you begin?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Left costal margin, pressing downward</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Starting at the left costal margin risks missing a massively enlarged spleen whose edge has already crossed the midline. Always begin from the right iliac fossa and advance toward the left costal margin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Epigastrium, moving toward the left flank</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The epigastrium is not the starting point for splenic palpation. The correct technique begins from the right iliac fossa and moves toward the left costal margin with each inspiration.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Right iliac fossa, advancing toward the left costal margin</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Starting from the right iliac fossa and advancing toward the left costal margin with each breath ensures you detect even a massively enlarged spleen whose edge may have crossed the midline.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Umbilical region, moving laterally to the left</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The umbilical region is too proximal a starting point. Begin from the right iliac fossa — a massively enlarged spleen can extend that far, and you must pick up its edge from below.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct technique is to begin from the right iliac fossa and advance toward the left costal margin with each inspiration. Starting distally ensures you detect even massive splenomegaly."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Splenic palpation begins distally at the right iliac fossa and advances toward the left costal margin. This ensures you do not miss a massively enlarged spleen that has crossed the midline."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
