          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient has shifting dullness in both flanks and a distended abdomen. Followin</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient has shifting dullness in both flanks and a distended abdomen. Following the principle of letting findings choose investigations, which initial workup is most appropriate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Erect chest X-ray followed by contrast-enhanced CT</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>An erect chest X-ray targets pneumoperitoneum in a peritonitic abdomen, not ascites. Shifting dullness indicates free fluid — the correct pathway is ultrasound and a diagnostic ascitic tap.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Full blood count, serum amylase, and urgent surgical referral</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This combination targets a peritonitic or acute abdomen. Shifting dullness indicates ascites, which calls for ultrasound to confirm the fluid and a diagnostic tap to analyse it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ultrasound abdomen and diagnostic ascitic tap</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ascites confirmed by shifting dullness calls for ultrasound to quantify the fluid, followed by a diagnostic tap. Analysing the fluid for cell count, protein, cytology, and culture directly narrows the differential.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Contrast-enhanced CT scan as the sole initial investigation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Jumping to CT alone skips the targeted, less invasive first steps. The findings-driven approach for ascites is ultrasound first, then a diagnostic tap — the fluid analysis itself provides diagnostic information that CT cannot.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Shifting dullness confirms ascites. The findings-driven investigation pathway for ascites is ultrasound to confirm and quantify the fluid, followed by a diagnostic ascitic tap — sending fluid for cell count, protein, cytology, and culture."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The findings-driven approach matches ascites to ultrasound plus diagnostic tap. Fluid analysis for cell count, protein, cytology, and culture directly narrows the differential diagnosis."
                }
              ],
